export const scalingNodejsRedis = "## Introduction\n\n**When our order dashboards started timing out during peak retail hours, the fix wasn't more Node processes — it was getting Node to stop doing the same expensive work over and over.**\n\nNode.js is excellent at handling thousands of concurrent connections on a single thread, right up until that thread is stuck waiting. In a typical order-management system, two things stall it: hammering the primary database for the same \"hot\" rows on every request, and doing slow, blocking work (PDF generation, third-party API calls, email) inline with the request. Redis solves both. This article walks through how we used Redis as a cache, session store, rate limiter, job-queue backend, and pub/sub bus to keep our dashboards responsive under production load — with the pitfalls that bit us along the way.\n\n## Why Node scaling bottlenecks on hot reads and blocking work\n\nNode's event loop shines when work is I/O-bound and cheap to dispatch. It falls apart in two scenarios:\n\n- **Hot reads.** A dashboard that shows \"orders in the last hour\" might run the same aggregate query for every one of 500 concurrent viewers. Postgres can serve one such query in 40ms, but 500 of them queued behind a connection pool of 20 turn into seconds of latency and a saturated database.\n- **Blocking work.** Generating an invoice PDF or calling a shipping API takes 800ms–3s. Do it inside the request handler and you tie up an event-loop turn's worth of continuations, balloon your p99, and risk request timeouts.\n\nThe pattern is the same in both cases: the work is either **repeated** (cache it) or **deferrable** (queue it). Redis is an in-memory data store with sub-millisecond reads, making it the natural landing spot for both.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Before adding Redis, measure. If a query is slow because it's missing an index, cache the fix — add the index first. Redis should absorb <i>load</i>, not paper over unindexed queries.</p></div></div>\n\n## Redis as a cache: the cache-aside pattern\n\nThe workhorse pattern is **cache-aside** (lazy loading): the application checks Redis first, falls back to the database on a miss, then populates the cache with a TTL.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Request] --> B{In cache?}\n    B -- Yes --> C[Return cached]\n    B -- No --> D[Query Postgres]\n    D --> E[Write to Redis with TTL]\n    E --> C\n```\n\nHere it is with `ioredis`, which we prefer over `node-redis` for its cluster support and robust auto-reconnect:\n\n```ts\nimport Redis from \"ioredis\";\n\nconst redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!, {\n  maxRetriesPerRequest: 3,\n  enableReadyCheck: true,\n});\n\nconst TTL_SECONDS = 60;\n\nasync function getOrderSummary(merchantId: string) {\n  const key = `order-summary:${merchantId}`;\n\n  const cached = await redis.get(key);\n  if (cached !== null) {\n    return JSON.parse(cached); // cache hit\n  }\n\n  const summary = await db.query(\n    `SELECT status, count(*) FROM orders\n     WHERE merchant_id = $1 AND created_at > now() - interval '1 hour'\n     GROUP BY status`,\n    [merchantId]\n  );\n\n  // SET with expiry in one round-trip; NX-free because we always overwrite\n  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(summary), \"EX\", TTL_SECONDS);\n  return summary;\n}\n```\n\nA 60-second TTL on our dashboard summary cut database load by roughly 95% during peak, because 500 viewers refreshing every few seconds now share one cached result per minute.\n\n### Choosing TTLs\n\nTTL is a bluntness-vs-freshness dial. Match it to how stale the data may be:\n\n| Data | Suggested TTL | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Dashboard aggregates | 30–60s | Cheap to recompute, staleness tolerable |\n| Product catalog | 5–15 min | Changes rarely, high read volume |\n| User profile | Until write + invalidate | Correctness matters, so invalidate explicitly |\n| Auth session | Sliding, e.g. 30 min | Refresh on activity |\n\n## Cache invalidation and stampede protection\n\nThere are two hard parts here, and both bit us in production.\n\n**Invalidation on write.** For data that must be fresh, don't rely on TTL alone — delete (or rewrite) the key when the underlying row changes:\n\n```ts\nasync function updateOrderStatus(orderId: string, status: string, merchantId: string) {\n  await db.query(`UPDATE orders SET status = $1 WHERE id = $2`, [status, orderId]);\n  // Invalidate derived caches so the next read recomputes\n  await redis.del(`order-summary:${merchantId}`);\n}\n```\n\n**Cache stampede** (a.k.a. thundering herd) is the nastier one. When a hot key expires, every concurrent request misses simultaneously and slams the database at once — the exact spike caching was meant to prevent. The fix is a short-lived lock so only one request recomputes while others briefly wait or serve stale data:\n\n```ts\nasync function getWithStampedeGuard(key: string, ttl: number, loader: () => Promise<unknown>) {\n  const cached = await redis.get(key);\n  if (cached !== null) return JSON.parse(cached);\n\n  const lockKey = `lock:${key}`;\n  // SET NX with a short PX acts as a mutex; only one caller wins\n  const gotLock = await redis.set(lockKey, \"1\", \"PX\", 5000, \"NX\");\n\n  if (!gotLock) {\n    // Someone else is recomputing; wait briefly and re-read\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));\n    const retry = await redis.get(key);\n    if (retry !== null) return JSON.parse(retry);\n  }\n\n  try {\n    const fresh = await loader();\n    await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(fresh), \"EX\", ttl);\n    return fresh;\n  } finally {\n    await redis.del(lockKey);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Setting the same TTL on thousands of keys populated at deploy time means they all expire in the same second. Add jitter — e.g. <code>ttl + Math.floor(Math.random() * 15)</code> — so expirations spread out.</p></div></div>\n\n## Redis for session storage\n\nStoring sessions in-process breaks the moment you run more than one Node instance behind a load balancer — a user's session lives on whichever box first served them. Externalizing sessions to Redis makes your app stateless and horizontally scalable.\n\n```ts\nimport session from \"express-session\";\nimport { RedisStore } from \"connect-redis\";\nimport Redis from \"ioredis\";\n\nconst redisClient = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!);\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    store: new RedisStore({ client: redisClient, prefix: \"sess:\" }),\n    secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET!,\n    resave: false,\n    saveUninitialized: false,\n    cookie: { httpOnly: true, secure: true, maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 30 },\n  })\n);\n```\n\nSessions get a natural TTL from `maxAge`, and any instance can serve any user. This is also what lets you deploy rolling updates without logging everyone out.\n\n## Redis for rate limiting\n\nRate limiting needs a shared counter across all instances — again, per-process state fails. Redis atomic increments make this trivial. A fixed-window limiter:\n\n```ts\nasync function isRateLimited(userId: string, limit = 100, windowSec = 60) {\n  const key = `ratelimit:${userId}:${Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000 / windowSec)}`;\n  const count = await redis.incr(key);\n  if (count === 1) {\n    await redis.expire(key, windowSec); // set TTL only on first hit\n  }\n  return count > limit;\n}\n```\n\nThe `INCR`/`EXPIRE` pair is nearly atomic, but there's a subtle race: if the process dies between them, the key never expires. For production, wrap both in a Lua script (which Redis runs atomically) or use a sliding-window log with a sorted set. The `rate-limiter-flexible` library implements both correctly and is worth reaching for.\n\n## Offloading slow work to background job queues\n\nThis is where Redis earns its keep for blocking work. Instead of generating an invoice inside the request, we push a job and return immediately; a separate **worker** process consumes it. **BullMQ** (built on Redis) is our queue of choice.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[API request] --> B[Add job to queue]\n    B --> C[Return 202 Accepted]\n    D[Worker process] --> E[Pull job from Redis]\n    E --> F[Generate PDF]\n    F --> G[Store result and notify]\n```\n\nProducer side, inside the request:\n\n```ts\nimport { Queue } from \"bullmq\";\n\nconst invoiceQueue = new Queue(\"invoices\", {\n  connection: { host: \"redis\", port: 6379 },\n});\n\napp.post(\"/orders/:id/invoice\", async (req, res) => {\n  await invoiceQueue.add(\n    \"generate\",\n    { orderId: req.params.id },\n    { attempts: 3, backoff: { type: \"exponential\", delay: 2000 }, removeOnComplete: 1000 }\n  );\n  res.status(202).json({ status: \"queued\" });\n});\n```\n\nWorker side, in a **separate process** so heavy work never touches the API event loop:\n\n```ts\nimport { Worker } from \"bullmq\";\n\nconst worker = new Worker(\n  \"invoices\",\n  async (job) => {\n    const pdf = await buildInvoicePdf(job.data.orderId); // slow, blocking work\n    await uploadToStorage(pdf);\n  },\n  { connection: { host: \"redis\", port: 6379 }, concurrency: 5 }\n);\n\nworker.on(\"failed\", (job, err) => {\n  logger.error({ jobId: job?.id, err }, \"invoice job failed\");\n});\n```\n\nBullMQ gives you retries with exponential backoff, delayed jobs, rate-limited workers, and a dead-letter path for jobs that exhaust their attempts — all persisted in Redis so nothing is lost on a deploy.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>Run workers as their own deployable service, not a thread inside your API. You can then scale worker replicas independently — bursts of invoice jobs shouldn't require scaling your web tier.</p></div></div>\n\n## Pub/sub for real-time updates\n\nOnce orders are processed asynchronously, the dashboard needs to know when something changes. Redis Pub/Sub broadcasts events to every subscribed instance, so a WebSocket client connected to instance A gets updates triggered on instance B.\n\n```ts\nconst publisher = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!);\nconst subscriber = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL!); // must be a dedicated connection\n\nawait subscriber.subscribe(\"order-events\");\nsubscriber.on(\"message\", (_channel, message) => {\n  const event = JSON.parse(message);\n  broadcastToWebSocketClients(event); // fan out to connected dashboards\n});\n\n// Elsewhere, when an order changes:\nawait publisher.publish(\"order-events\", JSON.stringify({ orderId, status: \"shipped\" }));\n```\n\nA key rule: a connection in subscriber mode can't run normal commands, so use **separate clients** for publishing and subscribing. Note that Pub/Sub is fire-and-forget — if a subscriber is down, it misses the message. For guaranteed delivery, use **Redis Streams** instead.\n\n## Pitfalls we hit in production\n\n**Memory eviction policy.** Redis is memory-bound. When it fills up, its behavior depends on `maxmemory-policy`. If you use Redis purely as a cache, set it to `allkeys-lru` so cold keys get evicted. If you also store sessions or queue data in the same instance that you can't afford to lose, `allkeys-lru` will happily evict those too — run a separate instance, or use `volatile-lru` (evicts only keys with a TTL).\n\n```bash\n# In redis.conf, for a cache-only instance:\nmaxmemory 2gb\nmaxmemory-policy allkeys-lru\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Mixing durable data (sessions, queues) and disposable cache in one Redis with an <code>allkeys</code> eviction policy. Under memory pressure Redis may evict a live session or an unprocessed job. Separate instances, or at minimum use <code>volatile-lru</code> and only TTL the cache keys.</p></div></div>\n\n**Connection pooling.** `ioredis` multiplexes commands over a single TCP connection, so you generally do **not** want a connection-per-request. Create one client (or a small number) and share it across your app. BullMQ, however, needs its own connections and expects `maxRetriesPerRequest: null` on the connection it uses — sharing your cache client with BullMQ will throw. Keep queue connections separate from cache connections.\n\n**Serialization cost.** `JSON.stringify`/`parse` on large objects is CPU work on the main thread. Cache narrow, purpose-built shapes rather than entire ORM entities, and consider `msgpack` for very hot, large payloads.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>Node bottlenecks on repeated hot reads and inline blocking work — cache the former, queue the latter.</li>\n<li>Cache-aside with sensible TTLs is the default pattern; add explicit invalidation on write for data that must stay fresh.</li>\n<li>Protect against cache stampedes with a short SET NX lock and add jitter to TTLs so keys don't expire in lockstep.</li>\n<li>Externalize sessions and rate-limit counters to Redis so your app stays stateless and horizontally scalable.</li>\n<li>Move slow work to BullMQ workers running as a separate service, with retries and backoff.</li>\n<li>Never mix durable data and disposable cache under an allkeys eviction policy, and share pooled connections rather than opening one per request.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Should I cache in Redis or just add a read replica?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>They solve different problems. A read replica spreads query load but still pays full query cost and adds replication lag. Redis serves a precomputed result in sub-millisecond time. For hot, repeated reads Redis is far cheaper; use replicas for scaling out varied read queries.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>How do I keep the cache consistent with the database?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Accept that caching trades some consistency for speed. Use short TTLs for tolerable staleness, and explicitly delete or rewrite keys on write for data that must be correct. Avoid write-through complexity unless you truly need read-your-writes guarantees.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Is BullMQ overkill if I only have a few background jobs?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>If you already run Redis, BullMQ adds little overhead and gives you retries, backoff, and persistence for free — things a naive setTimeout or in-memory queue won't survive a deploy. It's worth it the moment a job matters enough that losing it is a problem.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Can I run cache, sessions, queues, and pub/sub on one Redis instance?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>You can to start, but separate concerns as you grow. The biggest risk is the eviction policy: a cache-friendly allkeys-lru will evict sessions and jobs under memory pressure. At scale, run distinct instances (or Redis logical databases with careful policy) for durable versus disposable data.</p></div></details>\n";
